{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And Moses implored.</b> This root we have not found with this meaning except in connection with face, like יחלו פני נדיב (Proverbs 19:6). And in my opinion it is like ופניה לא היו לה עוד (1 Samuel 1:18), and all of them are thus. Therefore it is forbidden to say חליתיך or אחלך, for it would derive from the root of אשר חלה ד׳ בה (Deuteronomy 29:30). Some say that this prayer is the one mentioned in the portion והיה עקב, and this prayer was worthy to be written after Moses returned to the mountain; therefore וינחם ד׳ — after he prayed and prostrated himself forty days. And if He relented from the evil beforehand, what reason to say אולי אכפרה בעד חטאתכם (ch. 30)? For he went down and burned the calf and killed those who served it; then he returned to pray to HaShem on behalf of Israel and on behalf of Aaron, who was the cause. And others said that the prayer אל תשחת עמך (Deuteronomy 9:26) is not the one mentioned in this place. And in my opinion the reasons of the prayer are identical, as I shall explain: for how could he appease the face of HaShem before burning the calf, making the children of Israel drink, and killing those who served it? Rather, HaShem hinted to him that he should pray after his descent and removal of the calf; therefore אולי אכפרה בעד חטאתכם (ch. 30) is written after he removed the sin, as it is written ואת חטאתכם אשר עשיתם את העגל (Deuteronomy 9:21). Now since HaShem said to him ועתה הניחה לי, he mentioned the prayer that he prayed when he prostrated himself before HaShem, and this passage was worthy to be written after וישב משה אל ה׳ (ch. 31). And there is no earlier and later in the Torah; rather, he wished to join the matter of מי אשר חטא לי (ch. 33) with ועתה לך נחה את העם (ch. 34), and it is written ויגוף ד׳ את העם (ch. 35)." "<b>And he implored.</b> Now it is written ואתנפל לפני ד׳ את ארבעים היום ואת ארבעים הלילה וגו׳ כי אמר ד׳ להשמיד אתכם (Deuteronomy 9:25), and this is הרף ממני ואשמידם (Deuteronomy 9:14). For reason cannot bear that Moses would pray on behalf of Israel while the calf was among them, until he removed it." "<b>Now why, YHWH, would Your anger burn against Your people.</b> Like אל תשחת עמך ונחלתך (Deuteronomy 9:26) — אשר הוצאת בכחך הגדול ובזרועך הנטויה (Deuteronomy 9:29). And this is אשר הוצאת מארץ מצרים בכח גדול וביד חזקה. And it is written זכור לעבדיך לאברהם ליצחק וליעקב (Deuteronomy 9:27) — and this is זכור לאברהם ליצחק ולישראל עבדיך אשר נשבעת (below v. 13), and the meaning is that You should remember to them Your oath. And he did not explain this at זכור לעבדיך, for it is not like זכור עדתך קנית קדם (Psalms 74:2), because of the lamed. Now פן יאמרו הארץ אשר הוצאתנו משם (Deuteronomy 9) — this is like למה יאמרו מצרים לאמר (v. 12)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "Resolved ד׳ as HaShem throughout per voice rules. The closing chain of verse references (ch. 33–35) appears to point to the surrounding narrative in Exodus 32, not literal chapter 33–35 citations; translated as printed.", 1 "Resolved abbreviation ע״ג as עַל גַּל (upon the calf), reading ע״ג ביניהם as 'while the calf was among them.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:39:56.358067549Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}